	"name": "Moonblade",
	"type": "object",
	"img": "icons/weapons/polearms/halberd-crescent-glowing.webp",
	"system": {
		"description": "<p>Ironwood weaponry is fairly rare in most communities, but among cultures founded and populated primarily by the longer lived heritages, such as elves and gnomes, it appears more often. And the moonblade, a cunningly grown ironwood mobility weapon, is among the most prized of them all.</p><p>Typically the shaping of a moonblade happens when a parent learns that their child will have difficulties with their legs as they get older. Painstakingly carved and cultivated as a limb of the ironwood tree, the long blade, the supportive handle, and the arm cuff are maintained for years on end until the child finishes growing. Then the weapon is given as a gift when they’re old enough to take care of themself, and the weapon is the right length for their height.</p><p>While the upper portion of the weapon looks much like a forearm crutch, the length beyond the handle is a blade all the way to its finely tapered point. Able to be wielded as a push-blade or for powerful overhanded chopping motions, moonblades are generally grown as a pair, one for each arm, though they do not have the dual wielding or light properties, as one should be planted for support while the other is used for violent ends.</p><p>The moonblade was named after its most famous wielder, a dancing bard who would plant one moonblade into the ground to spin or twirl on its point, and who could practically run upon the blades themselves, with their feet never touching the ground, as they flew across the battlefield with a song in their throat and their foes strewn like cordwood on the ground.</p><p>Some other cultures make similar mobility weapons of steel, or iron-banded wood with a smaller blade to create an axe or spear style of moonblade. While they function the same for mechanical purposes they’re typically treated with less reverence.</p>",
